[17592186100929 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100929 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100929 #edb/ref 1039 "Sketching the Derivative of a Quadratic Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100929 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100929 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fa025d7b-74d5-5627-a7cd-12e3681b8b82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1039 "Sketching the Derivative of a Piecewise-Differentiable Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100930 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "97710476-c327-5b2d-9675-10f36b967d3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1045 "Sketching the Derivative of a Function From the Function's Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050166 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101061 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "752a2e8d-dfc4-5210-8dfe-31f383092e9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101301 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101301 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101301 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101301 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a4/a44ff10b8b99077e742024dc360563e2481871a44592f1fd9260947dba82dfed.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101301 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12ca6b93-de58-502b-8ecb-6b5fc6d679b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101301 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary points were identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, the function has an inflection point at $x≈1.5.$ So ${f}^{″}(1.5)≈0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of ${f}^{′}(1.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x≈1.5,$ the slope is negative.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/af/affedc1cf175ac4fb632345fcf45d5facbc29ecbe6ce5f4a2c31800a22e2e170.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101304 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101304 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20250" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101304 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101304 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7c/7c816445b7d8f5e06780337062f260a965407a440b43b3542c640c44232c4574.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Of the following, which could be the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101304 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50c220b6-4dcc-5cec-b906-7473e71c98bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101304 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-0.5)$ | $-0.5$ | $(-0.5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points.  Also, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ and $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](images/60/608c88c65eff4c63520a7a5a4a51a468601b0d6edcf5f063475c8429e1308962.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101604 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101604 #edb/ref 1061 1224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101604 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "beaa6f10-10cb-5300-9425-d5fce50a2dec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101604 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186102004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101605 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101605 #edb/ref 1061 1225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101605 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1ebc76df-237a-5efb-96f2-b3585e2b318b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101605 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101606 #edb/ref 1061 1229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101606 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fe6a977a-c724-5c50-9d38-b0a7dc613ae3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101606 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1061 25278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "538a0daa-f570-5287-b6c1-2fd7946ab4da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101771 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1061 25279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "826cab52-c133-5e02-bcc7-a04d9190cd1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101772 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we are given the graph of the function $f(x),$ as shown below. The function has critical points at $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ and an inflection point at $x=0.5.$\nHow can we sketch the graph of the derivative $f'(x)?$\n\n![](images/b3/b3fcc7a08d2a083567cc2de62c9bb0c6e9516552c8a2f08d2e814604f5ec84f8.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have $f'(x)=0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is increasing, we have $f'(x) > 0.$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f'(x) < 0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nUsing this information, we can identify the regions where $f'(x)$ should cross the $x$-axis. According to the table, these are points $(-1,0)$ and $(2,0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/e9/e98d21084e06d2a28509b0b68f449c5f42959ee1adad9b3dbf912885968a3b33.png)\n\nAdditionally, we are told that the function has an inflection point at $x = 0.5.$ So $f''(0.5)=0$ and the tangent to the graph of $y=f'(x)$ must be parallel to the $x$-axis.\n\nWe don't know the exact value of $f'(0.5)$ but from the graph of $y=f(x),$ we can conclude that at $x = 0.5$ the slope is negative. Therefore, we get a point below the $x$-axis:\n\n![](images/59/5994a9139fbd1c530692a2fb40b2fb1b4b7bd0b34b2bf7ff7194390542748977.png)\n\nFinally, connecting up the parts of the graph above, we get an approximate sketch of $f'(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/f2/f21fca7744d7196e457f0a9d4511f644a3cf1e8c61018c152fc74ea7f6999f3e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1065 641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "732e85fe-aec5-51f6-806c-68e476077346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102004 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505105 #edb/ref 1080 "images/2d/2dc2c5bc8ca4e173a3ee4b853aa85666b2761cebedfdb8ae7038b794ce86af1c.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505105 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505105 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00e44565-9c53-5bb8-9cc9-7639994a6273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505116 #edb/ref 1080 "images/50/5045b953f12139167ac6498f8d379b0b89379344a1e3f04f555d35c39e87248a.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505116 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505116 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "065c16e1-4085-50a6-bb78-007ef4782272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505128 #edb/ref 1080 "images/a6/a68eb6c7dc24329ad71732682f0b489e0315e058a3928db5cbbcb9182c46cab0.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505128 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 175921860454